= Introduction <sec:introduccion>

Digital transformation represents a critical strategic necessity for contemporary business competitiveness @oecd_measuring_dt_2019. Although more than 80% of organizations are actively accelerating their technological initiatives @bcg_flipping_odds_2020, systematic failure rates hover around 70% across industry and academic literature @sanchez_DTGEP_2022 @tabrizi_digital_2019. This failure is not primarily attributable to deficiencies in subsequent software development, but rather to flawed technological decisions during the pre-project phase, triggering a severe accumulation of technical debt that can compromise between 20% and 40% of the total value of an organization's technology estate @mckinsey_tech_debt_2020.

As formalized in the SWEBOK v4 guide @swebok_v4 within its _Software Engineering Economics_ knowledge area, software engineering is fundamentally a discipline of choice aimed at aligning technical feasibility with strategic business goals through the _Engineering Decision-Making Process_. The DTGEP (_Digital Transformation Governance Engineering Process_) framework @sanchez_DTGEP_2022 operationalizes this process by decomposing it into structured stages of maturity assessment, organizational asset elicitation, and formal prioritization.

However, the rigorous application of these frameworks faces a fundamental operational barrier: organizations possess a crucial intangible component (tacit knowledge, workflows, human capabilities, and business processes) that serves as the submerged foundation supporting software projects @sanchez_intangibles_2021. Multicriteria decision methods such as AHP (_Analytic Hierarchy Process_) @AHP_2001 require this information to be formally structured. In practice, the elicitation process for these assets lacks automation @swebok_v4: it relies entirely on the software engineer through extensive qualitative interviews with stakeholders. Due to the excessive time consumption and high human cost of this manual effort, organizations systematically oversimplify or bypass this stage altogether, selecting technologies without analytical grounding.

To overcome this operational bottleneck, this work introduces _AI-Driven DTGEP_, a methodology that integrates autonomous conversational agents based on Large Language Models (LLMs) under the _ReAct_ paradigm @yao_react_2023 @weng_llm_agents_2023 into pre-project decision-making. Building upon the DTGEP framework, the proposal adapts its sub-processes into an asynchronous model: the agent relieves the engineer in conducting the interview and capturing information, feeding in a decoupled manner the deterministic execution of formal decision algorithms: digital maturity diagnosis with ALTUS @sanchez_ALTUS_2020, deduction of intangible assets and technological artifacts with SIPAC @sanchez_sipac_2017, and hierarchical multicriteria prioritization using AHP @AHP_2001.

The primary contributions of this paper are:

1. _Methodological formalization of AI-Driven DTGEP:_ A methodology for the software pre-project phase that adapts decision processes into an asynchronous agent-assisted execution model.
2. _Decoupled software architecture:_ An agentic engine developed on LangGraph that enforces strict JSON schemas and hierarchical memory to govern the interview and feed deterministic algorithms.
3. _Comprehensive empirical validation and contrast study:_ A reproducible benchmark across four real-world organizations evaluating seven language models (open-weight and proprietary), complemented by a comparative study against direct prompting (unstructured LLM inference) that empirically demonstrates the necessity of formal agentic and algorithmic governance.

The remainder of this article is structured as follows: @sec:related_work reviews related literature and synthesizes the research gap; @sec:metodologia details the methodology and software architecture of AI-Driven DTGEP; @sec:evaluacion presents the experimental framework, the research questions, and the empirical findings; @sec:threats discusses threats to validity; and @sec:conclusiones summarizes our findings and outlines future directions.
